  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1204 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nThe current surface analysis shows high pressure centered over \nthe northern Plains, maintaining a cool northwest flow regime \nacross the western Great Lakes. Widespread low stratus continues \nacross northern Wisconsin with moisture trapped beneath a \nsubsidence inversion centered between 800mb and 750mb. Upstream, \nan amplifying northern stream shortwave trough digging out of \nwestern Canada will induce cyclogenesis over the central Plains \nbefore tracking into the Upper Midwest. This surface low will \nbring widespread rainfall and strong cold advection to Wisconsin \non Thursday and Friday.\n\nWidespread Rain Thursday Into Thursday Night: An amplifying \nshortwave and developing surface cyclone tracking northeast across\nthe Great Lakes will spread rain into central Wisconsin around \nmidday Thursday before reaching northeast Wisconsin in the \nafternoon. Robust dynamic forcing and moderate mid-level \nfrontogenesis interacting with a moisture plume featuring \nprecipitable water values 180 percent of normal will yield \nwidespread rainfall. NBM probabilistic guidance indicates a 60 to \n90 percent chance of 24-hour liquid accumulations exceeding 0.50 \ninches, with expected storm total rainfall between 0.50 and 1.00 \ninch. Rain will diminish in intensity late Thursday night as the \nsystem center exits to the northeast, but scattered showers are\nexpected to continue through much of Friday within strong cyclonic\nflow.\n\nStrong Friday Wind Gusts And Marine Gales: Strong cold air \nadvection surges across Wisconsin Friday morning on the backside \nof the departing cyclone, dropping 850mb temperatures over western\nLake Superior to -4 degrees C. Deep cyclonic flow combined with \nsteep boundary layer lapse rates will drive strong northwest wind \ngusts. NBM guidance shows a 40 to 70 percent chance of wind gusts\nexceeding 40 mph inland, while gale-force wind gusts and wave \nheights of 6 to 10 feet create hazardous marine conditions across \nGreen Bay and Lake Michigan. Winds will begin to subside late \nFriday afternoon as the pressure gradient relaxes.\n\nWidespread Friday Night Hard Freeze And Frost: Canadian surface \nhigh pressure quickly building into Wisconsin Friday night will \nbring in very cold air beneath clearing skies and diminishing \nwinds. Overnight minimum temperatures will plummet into the \nmid-20s to lower 30s across north-central and central Wisconsin, \nwith mid-30s near the Lake Michigan shore. NBM probabilistic \nguidance displays high confidence in a widespread hard freeze, \nshowing a greater than 90 percent chance of temperatures below 33 \ndegrees west of the Fox Valley and a 60 to 90 percent chance \neastward. Rapid solar heating Saturday morning will quickly burn \noff widespread frost, warming daytime temperatures into the mid to\nupper 50s.\n\nBreezy Weekend Warming Trend And Minimal Rain: Return southerly\nflow and warm advection strengthen Saturday afternoon into Sunday\nas surface high pressure shifts to the East Coast. Daytime high\ntemperatures will rebound into the 60s over the weekend and reach\nthe upper 60s by Tuesday under mostly sunny skies. Tightening\nregional pressure gradients will produce breezy southwest winds,\nwith NBM guidance indicating a 50 to 70 percent probability of\ngusts reaching 30 to 35 mph on Saturday and Sunday. High\nconfidence exists in minimal precipitation coverage with NBM\n24-hour rainfall probabilities remaining under 10 percent through\nmidweek.",
